index 000000000..0675b7c4f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/website/docs/api/native-mlx-video.md
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+# Native MLX-Serve H3 video
+
+`api_format: mlx_video`, provider `mlx-serve`, catalog protocol `mlx/video@1`,
+and wire format `mlx.video.v1` route `POST /v1/video/generations` through native
+SR capability/recipe selection. This contract targets MLX-Serve 26.9.6 and the
+MiniMax-H3 FL2VA pack. H3 runs on a macOS Metal host; the SR/Envoy gateway runs
+in the Linux Kubernetes VM.
+
+Requests accept `model`, `prompt`, `stream`, `width`, `height`, `num_frames`,
+`steps`, `seed`, `turbo`, `fast`, `chain_windows`, `first_frame_image`,
+`last_frame_image`, `preview`, `preview_frames`, and `preview_max_side`.
+Keyframes must be base64 PNG/JPEG. Dimensions are multiples of 32, up to 2048;
+steps are 1–1000, with a minimum of 4 for turbo. Chain windows are 1–6.
+Unsupported fields, host-local LoRA paths, reference-partition inputs and chat
+output budgets are rejected before backend dispatch. Model capability cards
+must explicitly declare image input for keyframes and video/audio output.
+
+The response is the engine's JSON containing base64 `rgb8` frames and
+`pcm_s16le` audio. With `stream: true`, progress SSE ends in a `type: complete`
+payload. SR preserves this wire; it does not create video jobs or transcode
+media. The caller decodes/muxes frames and PCM using the returned frame count,
+FPS and audio metadata. H3 may snap requested frames to its native temporal
+ladder; callers must use the returned count.
+
+`auto`, `local-only`, and `cloud-only` retain normal entrypoint/recipe rules.
+Physical model names and client-supplied routing headers cannot bypass them.
+Cross-wire projection into chat, speech or Omni video is rejected. Successful
+native media responses bypass ExtProc body processing, keeping large video
+payloads out of the router's buffered codec. Envoy applies an explicit
+per-model timeout; service lifecycle remains an operator action in project A.
+
+Upstream contract: https://github.com/ddalcu/mlx-serve/tree/v26.9.6
diff --git a/website/docs/api/native-multipart.md b/website/docs/api/native-multipart.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..82fb8a332
--- /dev/null
+++ b/website/docs/api/native-multipart.md
@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+---
+title: Multipart speech recognition and image edits
+---
+
+`/v1/audio/transcriptions`, `/v1/audio/translations`, and `/v1/images/edits`
+are distinct native tasks. Their `api_format` values are `transcription`,
+`translation`, and `image_edit`. Model cards declare `audio_input` plus
+`transcription` or `translation`; image editing requires `image_input` and
+`image_editing`. Having the Chat audio-input capability does not imply ASR.
+All requests still use public entrypoints and native candidate selection.
+
+These endpoints require multipart/form-data and its boundary. The codec parses
+at most 16 MiB, 64 parts, 16 files, and 64 KiB per form-text value, without writing
+uploads to disk. File names are metadata, never local paths. Only known fields
+are accepted; duplicate single-value fields, malformed boundaries, empty files,
+and invalid controls fail explicitly. The selected provider model replaces the
+public model field when the codec rebuilds the form. File bytes, names, MIME
+types, repeated array fields, and supported task parameters are retained.
+
+The typed form inventory and validation are in `protocolcodec/multipart_fields.go`.
+ASR supports the pinned vLLM sampling and stream form extensions, including an
+explicit `max_completion_tokens` when using strict context limits. Image edits
+use prompt bounds without a text output-token reserve. Provider-specific field
+support still depends on the configured backend and model. The pinned Omni
+image-edit API accepts b64 output; other providers' formats require their own
+acceptance evidence. Unknown or unsupported provider responses remain errors.
+
+JSON, plain text, SRT/VTT, image responses, backend errors, and SSE pass through
+the same-wire media transport. The router does not run a follow-up Chat request,
+fetch remote media, retry a started stream, or convert multipart to another API.
+The bounded upload contract is not continuous long-audio streaming; oversized
+uploads fail rather than buffering without a bound. Release reports distinguish
+mock protocol acceptance from actual model inference.
diff --git a/website/docs/api/native-realtime.md b/website/docs/api/native-realtime.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..bf80e96d3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/website/docs/api/native-realtime.md
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+# Native WebSocket inference sessions
+
+The gateway supports two distinct handshakes:
+
+- `GET /v1/realtime?model=auto&sr_input=audio&sr_output=text` (or `text,audio`)
+  selects the vLLM audio realtime protocol, `api_format: realtime`.
+- `GET /v1/audio/speech/stream?model=local-only` selects Omni streaming text
+  speech, `api_format: speech_stream`.
+
+Both require a valid RFC 6455 WebSocket upgrade and ordinary gateway
+Authorization. The `model` is an enabled public entrypoint. SR decodes the query
+into a typed session requirement, resolves its recipe, applies candidate task
+capabilities and the native decision/algorithm, and dispatches one handshake.
+The engine receives its provider model query and configured credential. SR-only
+selection parameters are removed. HTTP body plugins and multi-model execution
+are rejected for these sessions. No prompt or Chat request is fabricated.
+
+Models explicitly declare `realtime` plus their actual input/output capabilities.
+The vLLM protocol requires `audio_input` and optionally `audio_output`; Omni
+speech streaming requires `speech_generation` and `audio_output`. A Chat, Speech,
+or SSE endpoint is not a substitute. Handshake constraints guide candidate
+selection; they do not validate subsequent frames or enforce per-turn token
+budgets. Configure those limits in the selected engine.
+
+A native client reads `x-vsr-realtime-provider-model` on the successful 101
+response and uses that value in the engine's `session.update` or `session.config`
+frame. vLLM requires its loaded model name in frame configuration even when the
+handshake query contains a model. Browser WebSocket APIs cannot read arbitrary
+handshake response headers: use an engine with a compatible served alias or a
+native client that exposes the headers. No browser-transparent frame model
+rewriting is claimed.
+
+Envoy's WebSocket upgrade configuration retains gateway auth and ExtProc in the
+handshake filter chain. ExtProc sets request and response body processing to
+NONE. All subsequent native text/binary/control frames stay on that upstream
+connection without another SR selection, frame decoding, SSE conversion,
+fallback, retry, or hidden model call. Reconnection is a new selection; this is
+connection affinity, not resumable session storage. Backend errors before the
+upgrade remain HTTP errors. After upgrade, errors, cancellation and close follow
+the engine protocol. SR cannot promise native events unsupported by that engine.
+
+For vLLM, send `session.update`, `input_audio_buffer.append` (base64 PCM16 at
+16 kHz), and `input_audio_buffer.commit`; receive native transcription events.
+Omni may also emit `response.output_audio.delta` and audio completion events.
+For speech streaming send `session.config`, repeated `input.text`, and
+`input.done`; receive `audio.start`, binary audio and completion events. Another
+utterance may use the same connection, ending with `session.close`. These are
+separate engine contracts, not a universal frame schema.
+
+Gateway idle timeout is 120 seconds in the deployment example; the engine can
+apply shorter configuration/idle deadlines. Usage and frame counts belong to
+engine events; ordinary HTTP request metrics measure the handshake/session
+transport and do not manufacture per-turn accounting.
+
+Transport reference: [Envoy HTTP upgrades](https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/v1.37.3/intro/arch_overview/http/upgrades).
diff --git a/website/docs/api/native-speech.md b/website/docs/api/native-speech.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..b135c991b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/website/docs/api/native-speech.md
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+---
+title: Native speech synthesis through ExtProc
+---
+
+`POST /v1/audio/speech` decodes synthesis input directly into the protocol
+request's `SpeechGeneration` options. It does not invoke Chat Completions or
+orchestrate another model. A client that needs generated prose followed by
+speech makes two independent requests.
+
+The endpoint requires `application/json`, a nonempty `input`, and a public
+model entrypoint. Set `global.router.require_entrypoint: true` to reject
+concrete model aliases at the public boundary. Existing installations retain
+the previous behavior when this option is false. Entrypoints and isolated
+recipes still select the decision, then the existing selection algorithm
+filters candidates by protocol, `speech_generation`, and configured context
+requirements. A single candidate follows the same checks.
+
+Configure a speech backend with `api_format: speech`, a `vllm-omni` provider
+binding, its actual provider model ID, and declared model task capabilities.
+The provider definition describes HTTP transport support; it does not assert
+that every loaded Omni model can synthesize speech. `audio_output` alone does
+not satisfy `speech_generation`.
+
+The speech codec retains OpenAI synthesis fields and the explicitly typed
+Omni extensions in `pkg/llmprotocol/speech_generation.go`. Unknown fields,
+invalid formats, and incompatible streaming settings fail at ingress. Under
+`candidate_requirements.context: known_limits`, provide `max_new_tokens` so
+selection can account for the output budget.
+
+Same-wire responses pass through ExtProc in bounded chunks, preserving the
+backend's status, Content-Type, bytes, and SSE termination events. Cross-wire
+speech stream conversion is unsupported. Cancellation is owned by the Envoy
+HTTP stream; speech never acquires a separate retry or workflow loop.
+
+`openai/speech@1` and `openai/images@1` are registered catalog protocols.
+Registration and a provider's endpoint availability are distinct from codec,
+loaded-model, and end-to-end acceptance. The downstream project's release
+contract records those acceptance levels separately.
diff --git a/website/docs/api/native-video-tasks.md b/website/docs/api/native-video-tasks.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..ac7cde748
--- /dev/null
+++ b/website/docs/api/native-video-tasks.md
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# Native asynchronous video tasks
+
+`POST /v1/videos` uses the same bounded multipart fields and input modalities as
+the synchronous Omni video endpoint, with a distinct `video` provider format.
+The public `model` resolves to a recipe. Native capability and context checks run
+before the existing selection algorithm chooses the creating provider instance.
+
+`GET /v1/videos/{id}`, `GET /v1/videos/{id}/content` and
+`DELETE /v1/videos/{id}` enter SR's protocol state dispatch. They restore the
+creating model, backend, provider profile, instance identity and recipe. No new
+model selection, inference retry or cross-provider fallback occurs. Delete uses
+the native engine's cancellation/deletion semantics. Collection listing and
+query parameters are explicitly unsupported; SR does not aggregate engines.
+
+Configure `global.stores.media_bindings` with a Redis `address`, optional
+`password_env`, `database` and `tls`, a `ttl_seconds` from 60 through 604800,
+`max_total_bytes` of at least 65536, and an `instances` map from logical model
+alias to a stable instance identity. Every such model must have exactly one
+concrete endpoint. That endpoint must reach one instance or a backend with shared
+native task state. Separate replicas use separate aliases/endpoints; an ordinary
+load-balanced Service over process-local task stores is not supported.
+
+Bindings reuse SR's existing Redis scoped TTL store under the dedicated
+`vsr:media-binding:v1:` prefix. They contain provider identity and task IDs, never
+prompts, files or credential values. Redis must be private and persistent for
+restart durability; all SR replicas use the same store. The gateway must verify
+Authorization before ext_proc. The credential digest scopes task ownership, so
+changing caller credentials requires completing existing tasks before rotation.
+
+The returned opaque `video_sr_...` ID replaces only the native response `id`.
+Creation/query/delete JSON is limited to 64 KiB; other fields and HTTP status are
+preserved. Downloads and native errors stream unchanged. Unknown/expired tasks
+return 404, a changed backend or recipe returns 409, and unavailable state returns
+503. Storage is reserved before creation. If the engine creates a job but the
+binding commit fails, SR returns an explicit indeterminate-result error and does
+not retry creation or silently cancel the job. Operators can inspect the engine
+directly for recovery; SR never claims that an uncertain request did not execute.
+
+This API needs the optional Redis helper; it is not an in-memory, single-router
+binding implementation. Redis does not execute inference or business workflows.
diff --git a/website/docs/api/native-video.md b/website/docs/api/native-video.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..fe7bec1e3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/website/docs/api/native-video.md
@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+# Native video generation
+
+The patch adds the locked vLLM-Omni multipart endpoint `POST /v1/videos/sync`.
+A prompt-only request is text-to-video. Uploaded `input_reference`, repeated
+`input_references`, `control_reference`, and JSON URL reference fields remain
+native form inputs. Every reference becomes ordered semantic input for model
+capability and context checks. Reference URLs are not fetched by the Router.
+Provider-local `file_id` references are rejected because they have no global
+binding at this ingress. Images, audio and video inputs each require the
+corresponding model capability, in addition to `video_generation` and
+`video_output`; `generate_sound=true` also requires `audio_output`.
+
+Supported controls are model/prompt, seconds, size, width/height, fps/num_frames,
+aspect_ratio, short_edge, num_outputs_per_prompt, start_time_seconds, quality,
+negative_prompt, seed, diffusion step/guidance controls, generate_sound and
+sound_duration, frame interpolation controls, lora/extra_params, user and
+return_stage_metrics. Unknown fields and engine-local model paths are rejected.
+No default duration, resolution, sampling parameter or model is invented.
+The loaded model still owns its architecture-specific parameter constraints.
+
+Requests use the existing bounded multipart codec (16 MiB total, 64 parts,
+16 uploaded files, 64 KiB per text field). The sync MP4 response stays on native
+Envoy streaming transport, with original status, Content-Type, disposition and
+backend errors. There is no video buffering or response conversion in SR.
+The default gateway has a 120-second response timeout; longer generation needs
+an explicit operator setting or the asynchronous interface.
+
+Source: locked `vllm_omni/entrypoints/openai/video/generation/helpers.py` and
+`api_server.py` at the upstream commit in `upstream.lock.json`.
+Async job creation/query/download/delete and realtime sessions are separate
+protocol contracts; implementing sync does not establish their support.
